{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":8951253,"ns":0,"title":"Frankfurt cases","extract":"Frankfurt cases (also known as Frankfurt counterexamples or Frankfurt-style cases) were presented by philosopher Harry Frankfurt in 1969 as counterexamples to the principle of alternate possibilities (PAP), which holds that an agent is morally responsible for an action only if that person could have done otherwise.","description":"Philosophical argument","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}